course: dictionary
lesson: 2866
title: Power and presence: dominate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dominate means to have power or control over someone or something. «Napoleon's empire at its height controlled or dominated most of western and central Europe.» The words controlled or dominated sit side by side, so they mean much the same: the empire was in charge of those lands.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. The sentence talks about an empire, lands and a map being redrawn, so dominated must be about power. Test it: if you swap in controlled, the sentence still makes sense. That is the check to use.
explain: Dominate can also mean to be the biggest, strongest or most noticeable thing in a place. A tall tower can dominate a skyline, and in sport a team that plays much better than the other side for most of a match dominates it. Ask yourself what is being described and whether it is about power or about being the most noticeable.
fact: Dominate is a verb: dominate, dominates, dominated, dominating.
fact: «Dominate» means to have power or control over someone or something.
fact: «Dominate» can mean to be the tallest or most noticeable thing in a place, as in «dominate the skyline».
fact: In sport, «dominate» means to play much better than the other team for most of a game.
fact: Control and rule are close in meaning to «dominate», but «dominate» often suggests being the strongest force in the picture.
exercise: «Napoleon's empire controlled or dominated most of western and central Europe.» What does dominated mean here? | Painted | Walked across | Was born in | *Had power and control over
exercise: «A tall tower can dominate the skyline.» What does dominate mean here? | *Was the tallest and most noticeable thing in view | Was hidden from sight | Was closed for repairs | Fell down
exercise: «The home team dominated the match from the first whistle.» What does dominated mean? | Stopped playing early | *Played much better than the other team for most of the game | Sat on the bench | Lost every single point
test: «One topic can dominate the news for weeks.» What does dominate mean? | *Be the biggest, most important part of | Be cheaper than | Be missing from | Be sent away from
test: Which word is closest in meaning to dominate, to have power over? | Obey | Shrink | *Control | Forget
exam: Which sentence uses dominate to mean the most noticeable thing in a place? | Ahmed dominated his bread at breakfast. | *The tall tower dominates the city skyline. | The sock dominated under the bed.
